; ======================================================================
; math.pi4 - the desktop BASIC maths functions, in 32-bit binary float,
;            ON THE RASPBERRY PI 4
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;
;     EnableFloatingPoint                       ; REQUIRED - see below
;     XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/math.pi4"
;
;     Procedure Main()
;       Protected h.f
;       h = Sqr(25.0)                           ; 5.0
;       h = Sin(Radian(30.0))                   ; 0.5
;       h = ATan2(1.0, 1.0)                     ; 0.7853982  (45 degrees)
;       h = Pow(2.0, 10.0)                      ; 1024.0
;     EndProcedure
;
; Sqr Pow Sin Cos Tan ATan2 Log Exp Int Degree Radian Sign, plus the four
; special-value helpers NaN Infinity IsNaN IsInfinity that the domain
; answers below are stated in terms of.
;
; ======================================================================
; 2026-08-26: THE PREMISE OF THIS FILE CHANGED. READ THIS BEFORE THE
;             SECTION BELOW IT, WHICH IS NOW PARTLY HISTORY.
; ======================================================================
; THE BACKEND LEARNED HARDWARE FLOATING POINT. `a.f * b.f` now compiles
; to one `fmul s0, s0, s1` on the Cortex-A72's FPU. The refusal this
; whole library was written around is gone, and the section immediately
; below - "THIS TARGET HAS NO FLOATING-POINT ARITHMETIC" - describes the
; world as it was until that day. It is kept, not deleted, because it is
; the argument for why the software core exists and that argument is
; still load-bearing for the paragraph after next.
;
; WHAT HAS *NOT* HAPPENED YET, so nobody assumes it has: the bodies below
; still call MathFAdd, MathFMul and the rest rather than using operators.
; They are correct and they still build; they are simply slower than they
; need to be now. Converting them is the mechanical change the next
; section describes, and it is a separate piece of work from making
; floats work at all.
;
; ======================================================================
; > DO NOT DELETE THE *B CORE. IT IS NOW A TEST ORACLE.
; ======================================================================
; The next section says that the day the backend learns `.f`, everything
; under THE SOFT BINARY32 CORE should be deleted. THAT INSTRUCTION IS NOW
; HALF WRONG, and the half that is wrong matters more than the half that
; is right.
;
; Still right: the `.f`-facing WRAPPERS (MathFAdd, MathFSub, MathFMul,
; MathFDiv, MathFLt and the rest) and the call sites in the polynomials
; should go back to plain operators.
;
; NOW WRONG: the bit-pattern core underneath them - MathFAddB, MathFMulB,
; MathFDivB, MathFCmpB, MathFRound, MathFFromIntB, MathFToIntB - MUST
; STAY. It is correctly rounded, it takes and returns raw patterns as
; integers, and it therefore contains no `.f` at all, which makes it an
; INDEPENDENT IMPLEMENTATION of the same specification that the hardware
; now implements. That is what
; RaspberryPi4/Examples/Diagnostics/pi4FpGate.pi4 checks the silicon
; against, on the board, bit for bit, over signed zeros, subnormals,
; infinities, NaNs, ties to even and a random sweep.
;
; Deleting the reference implementation in the same change that
; introduces the thing it is meant to check is how a project ends up
; unable to tell which of the two halves is wrong. The core costs some
; ROM in a program that includes this file and nothing at all in one that
; does not.
;
; ONE BUG WAS FOUND IN THAT CORE while the gate was being written, before
; any hardware ran: MathFFromIntB(-9223372036854775808) did not return a
; wrong number, it HUNG - see the long note at that procedure. Fixed the
; same day. It is the clearest possible argument for keeping the oracle
; and for writing down what should happen before measuring what does.
;
; ======================================================================
; READ THIS SECOND: WHY THIS FILE IS NOT A COPY OF ANY OTHER MATH
; LIBRARY HERE. THIS DESCRIBES THE TARGET AS IT WAS BEFORE 2026-08-26.
; ======================================================================
; The Pi 4 backend refuses every floating-point IR node. Measured on
; 2026-08-25 on the shipped pmfc.exe, one line per probe:
;
;     a.f + b.f     A64 ASSEMBLER FATAL: floating-point IR reached the
;     a.f - b.f     Raspberry Pi 4 phase-1 emitter; .f is not
;     a.f * b.f     implemented yet
;     a.f / b.f
;     If a.f < b.f  ... the same message. Even the COMPARISON.
;     r.f = n.l     ... and the CONVERSIONS, both directions. This is
;     r.l = f.f     why MathFFromInt and MathFToInt exist below: `nf = n`
;                   in the RP2350 source is an I2F node and does not
;                   build here either.
;
; So `h = Sqr(3.0 * 3.0 + 4.0 * 4.0)`, the first line of the RP2350
; file's own usage example, does not build on this part. The example at
; the top of this file passes 25.0 instead, and that difference is the
; whole subject of this header.
;
; WHAT IS *NOT* REFUSED, and it is what makes this file possible: .f
; STORAGE. A .f local, a .f parameter, a .f array element, a .f return
; value and a fractional LITERAL all compile and all hold correct
; IEEE-754 binary32. Probed: storing 2.5 and -1.0 into a `Dim F.f[]` and
; reading the bytes back gives $40200000 and $BF800000, at a stride of
; four bytes, which is exactly right. The type exists. Only the ALU for
; it is missing.
;
; SO EVERY ARITHMETIC OPERATION IN THIS FILE IS DONE IN INTEGERS, on the
; bit pattern, by the MathF* core below - a software binary32 written in
; ordinary 64-bit integer code. The public API is unchanged: you pass
; .f, you get .f, and the function names, argument order and domain
; answers are the desktop BASIC's exactly as in the file this was ported
; from. What changed is one layer underneath, where
;
;     r = x - nf * 0.693359375
;
; became
;
;     r = MathFSub(x, MathFMul(nf, 0.693359375))
;
; and nothing else. The polynomials, the range reductions, the split
; constants and the domain table are the RP2350 file's, unaltered.
;
; ======================================================================
; THE SOFT CORE IS A STOPGAP AND SHOULD BE DELETED, NOT MAINTAINED
; ======================================================================
; This is worth being blunt about, because a library carrying its own
; floating-point runtime is a smell and it should not be allowed to
; become the norm. FLOAT LOWERING BELONGS IN THE EMITTER. On the RP2040
; and RP2350 it lives in the compiler's ARM float helpers, where the
; register allocator and the width analysis can see it; on this target
; it does not exist yet, and putting the whole desktop maths surface out
; of reach until it does was the worse of the two available answers.
;
; THE DAY THE BACKEND LEARNS .f, THE CHANGE HERE IS MECHANICAL AND THE
; FILE GETS SHORTER:
;
;   1. Delete everything under THE SOFT BINARY32 CORE except FloatBits
;      and FloatFromBits, which are public API and stay.
;   2. Rewrite each call back into an operator - MathFAdd(a, b) -> a + b,
;      MathFMul -> *, MathFSub -> -, MathFDiv -> /, MathFLt(a,b) ->
;      a < b, and so on. Every call site is one of those six shapes and
;      nothing else.
;   3. Re-measure. The answers should not move: the core below is
;      CORRECTLY ROUNDED (see PROVEN), so it computes the same binary32
;      an IEEE-754 unit would.
;
; Nothing else in this file knows the core exists. That is the point of
; keeping it behind the .f-facing wrappers instead of open-coding bit
; patterns through the polynomials.
;
; THE FILE NEXT DOOR TOOK THE OPPOSITE DECISION, and both are defensible,
; so the difference is recorded rather than smoothed over.
; string_f32.pi4 hit the same wall and did NOT write StrF/ValF: it ships
; the integer helpers, refuses by not defining the two routines, and says
; the build error naming them is the correct outcome. That is the right
; call THERE, because a number formatter has no correct answer to give
; and a plausible wrong one is the worst possible failure in an
; instrument. It is the wrong call HERE, because Sqr and Sin DO have
; correct answers that integer arithmetic can produce, and a maths
; library that refuses every function is not a library.
;
; WORTH KNOWING IF YOU ARE FINISHING string_f32.pi4: MathFAdd, MathFSub,
; MathFMul, MathFDiv, MathFFromInt, MathFToInt and the five relational
; wrappers below are public and correctly rounded, and StrF/ValF can be
; written on them today. That would make string_f32.pi4 depend on this
; file, which the house rule forbids a LIBRARY from arranging for itself
; - the MAIN file has to list both - so it is a decision for whoever owns
; that file, not something to be done from this one.
;
; WHAT IT COSTS TODAY, counted rather than guessed - instructions
; actually executed in the interpreter, with the 832-instruction program
; prologue subtracted:
;
;     one MathFMul(3.0, 7.0)      1751 instructions
;     one Sqr(25.0)              17354 instructions
;
; against ONE `fmul s` and ONE `fsqrt s` on the silicon. Roughly three
; orders of magnitude, and a large part of it is the call overhead of
; going through MathBitsOf/MathFromBits on every operand - which is the
; price of keeping the core behind an .f-shaped door so that it can be
; deleted cleanly later.
;
; IN ROM: a program with none of this is 104 bytes; including this file
; takes it to 28376, and calling Sqr as well to 28428. READ THE SECOND
; NUMBER CAREFULLY - the include costs the whole library whether you call
; one function or all of them, because this backend does not dead-strip
; unreferenced procedures. On a part with gigabytes that is a different
; kind of number than it was on a Pico, but it is still worth knowing.
;
; This is not a fast maths library and it is not pretending to be; it is
; a correct one on a part whose float unit the compiler cannot yet reach.
;
; ======================================================================
; REQUIRES
; ======================================================================
;   EnableFloatingPoint, in YOUR file, ABOVE the XIncludeFile line.
;
;   Includes no other file (house rule: a library never includes a
;   library - the MAIN file lists them). It needs none.
;
;   The pragma is not optional and the error you get without it does not
;   say so at first glance. This file contains about ninety fractional
;   literals. Without the pragma each one is typed as Q32.32 FIXED-POINT
;   - that is the default - and the build stops with a message about a
;   .f that names a line INSIDE THIS LIBRARY and mentions nothing you
;   wrote. Measured on this target: the message is "Using a .f float
;   requires EnableFloatingPoint", pointing at math.pi4.
;
;   .fx, .d and .dec are refused outright on this target as well, so a
;   missing pragma here is even more likely to produce a complaint about
;   a type you never asked for.
;
;   NO SYMBOL IN THIS FILE COLLIDES with anything else in
;   RaspberryPi4/Lib. Checked mechanically: every Procedure name, every #
;   constant and every file-level Global across all nineteen .pi4 files
;   present on 2026-08-25, diffed - zero collisions in all three
;   categories. The internals are all Math*-prefixed; the public names
;   are the desktop BASIC's; this file declares no file-level global.
;
;   That check matters more here than usual because this backend gives
;   every PROCEDURE LOCAL a static BSS symbol named `<procedure>_<local>`
;   - `sqr_y`, `exp_nf`, `mathfround_mm`. Two files defining a procedure
;   of the same name would therefore collide in their locals as well as
;   their entry points. The directory is being added to by other hands,
;   so repeat the check if you add a file.
;
; ======================================================================
; WHY THIS IS A SEPARATE FILE AND NOT PART OF A WIRING LIBRARY
; ======================================================================
; Everything here is binary32 and needs EnableFloatingPoint, and on this
; target it also drags in a software float runtime. A blink sketch that
; only wanted constrain() should not pay for either.
;
; It is also a different NAMESPACE with different rules. These are the
; desktop BASIC's names and the desktop BASIC's exact semantics, argument
; order included - ATan2 takes x FIRST, which is the reverse of the C
; function of the same name and is the single most likely thing to be
; got wrong here.
;
; ======================================================================
; Abs IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT
; ======================================================================
; Identifiers fold case, so an Abs(x.f) here would collide with the
; abs(v.i) that the wiring libraries define, for one function that is two
; lines to write inline:
;
;     If v < 0.0 : v = 0.0 - v : EndIf
;
; ... except that on THIS target those two lines do not compile either,
; because they are a float comparison and a float subtraction. Write
;
;     If MathFLt(v, 0.0) = 1 : v = MathFSub(0.0, v) : EndIf
;
; or, better, `v = MathFAbs(v)`, which is below and is exact: it clears
; one bit and touches nothing else.
;
; ======================================================================
; SINGLE PRECISION ONLY, AND WHAT THAT COSTS YOU
; ======================================================================
; The desktop BASIC computes these in DOUBLE and offers a .d overload.
; This language has .f and no .d on this target, so every function is
; binary32 in and binary32 out. binary32 carries about 7 significant
; decimal digits. A result that agrees with the desktop to 7 digits is
; agreeing as closely as the type can.
;
; Where a function CANNOT match the desktop it is written down here and
; in that function's own comment, never left to be discovered:
;
;   Int()    returns a 32-bit value; the desktop returns the machine's
;            native integer. Magnitudes above 2147483647 saturate here.
;            NOTE that .i on THIS target is 64-bit and could hold the
;            wider answer - Int() still returns .l and still saturates
;            at the 32-bit bound, deliberately, because changing the
;            range of a documented function during a port is how a
;            program that moved between two chips starts disagreeing
;            with itself. MathFToInt() below is the unclamped 64-bit
;            spelling for anyone who wants the wider answer here.
;   Sin Cos  refuse an argument beyond 12867 radians instead of returning
;   Tan      a plausible number. The desktop keeps answering; its answers
;            out there are wrong too, just quietly. See TRIG below.
;   Pow      for a non-integer exponent is exp(y*log(x)) and carries that
;            composition's error. An INTEGER exponent up to +/-64 takes a
;            repeated-squaring path instead. A NEGATIVE base is refused
;            past that bound - see Pow().
;
; ======================================================================
; DOMAIN ERRORS - EVERY ONE HAS A DEFINED, DOCUMENTED ANSWER
; ======================================================================
; These match the desktop BASIC, which was RUN to establish them rather
; than read out of a manual, and every row below was RE-RUN on this
; target - see PROVEN:
;
;     Sqr(-1)          NaN            Log(0)        -Infinity
;     Sqr(-0.0)        -0.0           Log(-1)       NaN
;     Sqr(+Inf)        +Infinity      Log(+Inf)     +Infinity
;     Exp(1000)        +Infinity      Exp(-1000)    0.0
;     Pow(0, 0)        1.0            Pow(0, -1)    +Infinity
;     Pow(-2, 3)       -8.0           Pow(-2, 0.5)  NaN
;     Pow(x, 0)        1.0 always     ATan2(0, 0)   0.0
;     Sign(NaN)        0              anything(NaN) NaN
;
; The two places this file DIFFERS from the desktop, both deliberate and
; both inherited from the RP2350 file rather than introduced here:
;
;   Int(NaN) is 0. The desktop returns the CPU's "integer indefinite"
;   (-9223372036854775808), which is a plausible-looking number that is
;   not an answer. 0 is not an answer either, but it is not a number
;   anyone will mistake for one.
;
;   Sin/Cos/Tan return NaN beyond 12867 radians. See TRIG.
;
; ======================================================================
; TRIG: THE RANGE LIMIT IS A REFUSAL, AND IT IS THE POINT
; ======================================================================
; Reducing an angle modulo pi/2 is where a float trig function actually
; goes wrong. pi/2 is carried here in THREE pieces so that each product
; n*piece is exact:
;
;     A = 1.5703125                    = 201 * 2^-7,  8 significant bits
;     B = 0.0004837512969970703125     11 significant bits
;     C = 0.0000000754979012640433     the remainder
;
; n*A stays exact while 201*|n| < 2^24 and n*B while 2029*|n| < 2^24. The
; second is the binding one: |n| < 8267, i.e. |x| < about 12986 radians.
; Past that the reduction quietly loses bits and the error grows with no
; outward sign at all, so beyond 12867 radians (2048 whole turns) these
; return NaN. A NaN propagates and can be tested for; a sine that is
; wrong in the fourth digit cannot.
;
; If you are accumulating an angle in a loop, wrap it as you go rather
; than letting it grow.
;
; ======================================================================
; NO FILE-LEVEL GLOBALS - AND WHAT THAT DOES *NOT* BUY ON THIS BACKEND
; ======================================================================
; Every value lives in a Protected local, as in the file this came from.
; THE RP2350 FILE GOES ON TO CLAIM THAT THIS MAKES THESE FUNCTIONS
; REENTRANT, so a square root can be taken inside an interrupt handler
; while the foreground is inside one. THAT CLAIM IS FALSE HERE AND IS
; WITHDRAWN RATHER THAN CARRIED OVER.
;
; Measured, by reading the emitted symbol table: this backend gives every
; parameter and every Protected local a FIXED STATIC ADDRESS in BSS -
; `sqr_y`, `mathfround_mm`, `exp_nf` and the rest are all real symbols in
; the .sym file - and the only thing that goes on the stack is x30. A
; Protected local here IS a global; it merely has a name nobody else can
; spell.
;
; Recursion is a LOUD refusal and not a wrong answer: the frontend stops
; with "Recursion is not supported on this backend... it does not crash,
; it returns a plausible wrong number", confirmed on a two-line probe.
; Nothing in this file recurses. But DO NOT CALL THESE FROM AN INTERRUPT
; HANDLER while the foreground may be inside one; nothing checks it and
; the result is a wrong number in both. That is a property of the
; backend, not of this file, and it applies to every .pi4 library here.
;
; ======================================================================
; PROVEN - 2026-08-25
; ======================================================================
; Builds clean for -t pi4 (--load-addr 0x200000, -S), and then EXECUTED
; in the project's own A64 interpreter, tools/a64/a64_interp.py.
;
; THE SOFT CORE IS CORRECTLY ROUNDED, and that is asserted rather than
; hoped for. 1700 operand pairs - 400 of them every combination of
; twenty special patterns (+/-0, +/-Inf, +/-NaN, the smallest and
; largest subnormals, the smallest and largest normals, 1.0, pi, ...),
; 600 uniformly random 32-bit patterns, 300 pairs with exponents within
; three of each other where cancellation is worst, and 400 subnormal-
; heavy - run through add, subtract, multiply, divide, compare, integer-
; to-float and float-to-integer and compared against numpy's float32:
;
;     11900 assertions, 0 failures, 15218554 interpreter steps.
;
; Every result is BIT-IDENTICAL to the IEEE-754 answer, subnormals and
; signed zeros included. NaN payloads are canonicalised before comparing,
; which is the one thing not promised. Random patterns alone would not
; have been enough: the pairs with near-equal exponents are the ones
; that exercise the cancellation path in MathFAddB, and the subnormal-
; heavy sets are the only ones that reach the gradual-underflow loop in
; MathFRound at all.
;
; THE FUNCTIONS THEMSELVES were then checked two ways at once, which is
; the part that matters:
;
;   1. AGAINST A MODEL OF THE SAME ALGORITHM. A Python transcription of
;      each function, doing the identical sequence of float32 operations
;      in the identical order, must produce the IDENTICAL BIT PATTERN.
;      This is what proves the TRANSLITERATION - that MathFSub(a, b)
;      landed where `a - b` was and not where `b - a` was, that no
;      operand pair got swapped, that no Horner step went missing. A
;      single misplaced call shows up as a wrong bit pattern immediately,
;      on almost every argument, which is why this and not the ULP figure
;      is the check that actually guards the port.
;
;        1465 bit-identity assertions, 0 failures, 46559256 steps.
;
;      Per function, and every set includes +/-0, +/-Infinity, NaN, the
;      smallest and largest subnormals, the smallest and largest normals
;      and +/-pi as well as the random arguments:
;
;        Sqr 169   Exp 165   Log 168   Sin 164   Cos 161   Tan 124
;        ATan2 131 pairs     Pow 154 pairs       Int 99
;        Degree 42   Radian 43   Sign 15   IsNaN 15   IsInfinity 15
;
;   2. AGAINST THE TRUE VALUE, in ULP, using the host's double-precision
;      library as the reference.
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  MEASURED ACCURACY ON THIS TARGET - worst-case ULP against the true
;  value, over exactly the arguments above. Produced by the gate; NOT
;  copied from the RP2350 file, whose figures are close but not the same
;  and which measured a different argument set.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;      Sqr      0.75 ULP    1e-30 .. 1e+30, log-uniform
;      Exp      1.00 ULP    -87 <= x <= 88
;      Log      1.02 ULP    1e-30 .. 1e+30, log-uniform
;      Sin      1.12 ULP    |x| <= 6.3
;      Cos      1.05 ULP    |x| <= 6.3
;      Tan      1.54 ULP    |x| <= 1.4, excluding |cos x| < 0.01
;      ATan2    1.98 ULP    magnitudes to 1e6, all four quadrants
;      Pow      6.06 ULP    whole-number exponent, |y| <= 20
;               39.99 ULP   general path, base 0.01..50, |y| <= 20
;
;  A correctly rounded result is at most 0.5 ULP from the truth, so
;  everything at or near 1 ULP above is one rounding step of accumulated
;  polynomial error and nothing more. Pow's general path is the
;  composition Exp(y*Log(x)) and its error is dominated by y multiplying
;  Log's - see Pow(), where that is the whole reason the whole-number
;  path exists.
;
; THE DOMAIN-ERROR TABLE ABOVE WAS RE-RUN ON THIS TARGET, every row, plus
; the cases that are easy to get wrong and are not in it: Sqr and Log of
; the smallest subnormal (3.7433921e-23 and -103.27893, both through the
; 2^40 scaling path), Exp(-90) = 8.194009e-40 rather than 0, FloatBits
; staying signed, and Int() saturating at both ends.
;
;     62 domain assertions, 0 failures.
;
; THE EMITTED ASSEMBLY WAS READ for the three things that had to be true
; of an integer float library on a 64-bit part:
;   * NO FLOATING-POINT INSTRUCTION IN THE IMAGE AT ALL - zero fadd,
;     fmul, fdiv, fcmp, scvtf, fcvtzs or `ldr s`. The build would have
;     failed if one had crept in, but the count is worth having.
;   * THE SIGNIFICAND PRODUCT IS A FULL 64-BIT MULTIPLY: `mul x13, x11,
;     x12`. A `mul w` there would have thrown away the top of every
;     24x24 product, and the results would have been wrong in their low
;     bits with nothing to announce it.
;   * THE QUOTIENT AND REMAINDER ARE `sdiv x` and `msub x`, not their W
;     forms, so the 54-bit pre-shifted numerator survives the divide.
;   The only W registers in the whole image are the one `ldr w` in
;   MathBitsOf, the one `str w` in MathFromBits - the 32-bit accesses
;   that SHOULD be narrow - and sixteen `sxtw` belonging to the .l locals
;   and returns, which is the signed 32-bit type behaving as documented.
;
; NOTHING IN THIS FILE HAS RUN ON SILICON. Two independent instruction
; models agreeing, plus a reference implementation agreeing bit for bit,
; is the strongest evidence it has. It is not the same thing as a board.
;
; RE-CHECKED 2026-08-27 AND STILL TRUE OF THIS FILE, and dated twice on
; purpose: an undated "nothing has run" reads as present tense for ever.
; One caller EXISTS now and has not been run -
; RaspberryPi4/Examples/Diagnostics/pi4FpGate.pi4 includes this file
; specifically to race the soft binary32 core here against the hardware
; FPU, bit pattern for bit pattern, on the board. No bench record of
; that program running has been found in the vault or the git history.
; IF SOMEBODY RUNS pi4FpGate, THIS PARAGRAPH IS THE ONE TO COME BACK
; AND CHANGE - and note that pi4FpState must be run first, because a
; closed CPTR_EL2.TFP trap turns the first `fmul` into a silent board.
;
; ~~No image from this compiler has booted a Pi 4.~~ FALSE, and already
; false when it was written. First light was 2026-08-24; since
; 2026-08-26 the firmware loads KERNEL8.IMG and the monitor is
; self-hosting with HDMI, PCIe, USB storage, FAT32, gigabit Ethernet,
; the MMU and a Wi-Fi radio. The identical clause was pasted into
; bits.pi4 and sort.pi4 as well and went stale in all three at once -
; see bits.pi4's header for why a file should only make claims about
; itself and leave project-wide status to the vault.
;
; ONE THING THIS FILE SAYS ABOUT THE COMPILER HAS MOVED. The block below
; opens "Everything in this block exists only because the emitter cannot
; do float arithmetic yet." FLOAT SUPPORT LANDED IN THE COMPILER ON
; 2026-08-26. The soft core is not therefore dead - it became the ORACLE
; the hardware path is checked against, which is a better job than the
; one it was written for, and pi4FpGate exists to use it that way. Read
; "cannot do float arithmetic yet" below as history, not as status.
; ======================================================================


; ======================================================================
;  THE SOFT BINARY32 CORE
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Everything in this block exists only because the emitter cannot do
;  float arithmetic yet. See the header: when it can, this block goes
;  and the call sites become operators again.
;
;  THE REPRESENTATION. A "bits" value is an .i holding the 32-bit
;  IEEE-754 binary32 pattern, UNSIGNED, 0..4294967295. It is unsigned on
;  purpose and it is why PeekN exists in MathBitsOf: the sign bit of a
;  float is DATA to this code, not a sign, and every classification below
;  is a range test that would break if bit 31 arrived as a minus.
;
;  THE INTERNAL EXPONENT CONVENTION, used by every routine here: a value
;  is (-1)^s * m * 2^(e-23), where m is the significand as an INTEGER.
;  For a normal number m is in [2^23, 2^24). Subnormals are NORMALISED on
;  unpacking - shifted up until m reaches 2^23, with e decremented to
;  match - so that every nonzero input reaching the arithmetic has the
;  same shape. That single decision is what keeps the multiply and the
;  divide free of subnormal special cases: without it a subnormal divisor
;  makes the quotient loop produce a zero with a sticky bit, which is the
;  one case the rounder cannot recover.
;
;  WHY 64-BIT INTEGERS MAKE THIS EASY, and it is the one place where this
;  target's word size is a gift rather than a hazard: a 24x24 significand
;  product is 48 bits and fits EXACTLY, with no splitting; a division can
;  afford a 30-bit pre-shift and still leave room; and the addition can
;  align both operands in a 57-bit field and lose nothing. On a 32-bit
;  part every one of those needs a double-word helper. This file would be
;  three times its length on a Pico.
; ======================================================================

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  MathBitsOf(x) - the bit pattern of x, UNSIGNED, 0..4294967295.
;
;  PeekN and not PeekL. PeekL SIGN-EXTENDS on this target as of
;  2026-08-25, when .n arrived and retired PeekL's one-day exemption from
;  the Peek family's signedness rule, so PeekL(@t) of any NEGATIVE float
;  would arrive here as a negative .i and every `>= 2147483648` test in
;  this file would be false. FloatBits() below is the signed spelling and
;  is public; this one is the raw pattern and is internal.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i MathBitsOf(x.f)
  Protected t.f
  t = x
  ProcedureReturn PeekN(@t)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  MathFromBits(b) - the binary32 whose pattern is b. The mask means a
;  caller may hand this either spelling of the same pattern: 4286578688
;  and -8388608 are the same four bytes and both give -Infinity.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.f MathFromBits(b.i)
  Protected t.f
  PokeN(@t, b & $FFFFFFFF)
  ProcedureReturn t
EndProcedure

; --- classification, on a pattern ------------------------------------
Procedure.i MathFIsNaNB(b.i)
  If ((b >> 23) & 255) <> 255
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If (b & 8388607) = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

Procedure.i MathFIsInfB(b.i)
  If ((b >> 23) & 255) <> 255
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If (b & 8388607) <> 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

Procedure.i MathFIsZeroB(b.i)
  If (b & 2147483647) = 0
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

Procedure.i MathFSignB(b.i)
  ProcedureReturn (b >> 31) & 1
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  MathFMantB / MathFExpB - the significand and the exponent, under the
;  convention at the top of this block. Subnormals are normalised here so
;  that nothing downstream has to know they existed. Zero gives 0 and 0,
;  and every caller tests for zero before asking.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i MathFMantB(b.i)
  Protected e.i
  Protected m.i
  e = (b >> 23) & 255
  m = b & 8388607
  If e <> 0
    ProcedureReturn m | 8388608
  EndIf
  If m = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  While m < 8388608
    m = m << 1
  Wend
  ProcedureReturn m
EndProcedure

Procedure.i MathFExpB(b.i)
  Protected e.i
  Protected m.i
  Protected k.i
  e = (b >> 23) & 255
  m = b & 8388607
  If e <> 0
    ProcedureReturn e - 127
  EndIf
  If m = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  k = -126
  While m < 8388608
    m = m << 1
    k = k - 1
  Wend
  ProcedureReturn k
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  MathFRound(s, e, m, sticky) - pack (-1)^s * m * 2^(e-23) into a
;  binary32, ROUNDED TO NEAREST WITH TIES TO EVEN. This is the only place
;  in the file that rounds, which is why it is the only place that has to
;  be got exactly right.
;
;  `sticky` says "there is more nonzero value below m that you were not
;  given". The multiply never sets it (a 24x24 product is exact); the
;  divide sets it from a nonzero remainder; the add sets it from operand
;  bits shifted out during alignment.
;
;  PRECONDITION: m MUST NOT BE NEGATIVE. The sign is carried in `s`; `m`
;  is a magnitude. Written down here as of 2026-08-26 because it was
;  previously only implied, and one caller violated it: MathFFromIntB
;  took `0 - n` of the one 64-bit value whose negation wraps, handed the
;  negative result in here, and this procedure HUNG. The `up` loop is
;  where it happens - `mm < 8388608` is true for every negative value,
;  and `mm << 1` on a negative power of two reaches exactly zero, after
;  which the loop can never make progress and the exponent runs away.
;
;  It is deliberately NOT guarded here with a test and a substituted
;  answer. There is no correct binary32 to return for "a mantissa that
;  cannot exist", so a guard could only invent one, and an invented
;  answer in a rounder is worse than a hang: the hang is at least
;  visible. The precondition belongs to the four call sites, all of
;  which are in this file, and all of which now provably satisfy it.
;
;  THE THREE LOOPS, in the order they must run:
;    down   - while m >= 2^24, halve it. The bit falling off becomes the
;             guard; the PREVIOUS guard falls into sticky. Losing that
;             ordering is the classic double-rounding bug.
;    up     - while m < 2^23, double it, pulling the guard bit back in on
;             the first step. Only cancellation in the adder gets here,
;             and cancellation never leaves a sticky.
;    denorm - while the exponent is below -126, halve again. This is what
;             produces gradual underflow instead of flushing to zero, and
;             it is why Exp(-90) is 8.2e-40 here rather than 0.
;
;  Then one rounding decision, one possible carry out of it, and the
;  overflow test AFTER the carry - because rounding 0x1.fffffep127 up is
;  exactly how a finite input becomes an infinity.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i MathFRound(s.i, e.i, m.i, sticky.i)
  Protected g.i
  Protected st.i
  Protected ee.i
  Protected mm.i
  st = sticky
  ee = e
  mm = m
  g = 0
  If mm = 0
    If st = 0
      ProcedureReturn s << 31
    EndIf
    ProcedureReturn (s << 31) | 1
  EndIf
  While mm >= 16777216
    st = st | g
    g = mm & 1
    mm = mm >> 1
    ee = ee + 1
  Wend
  While mm < 8388608
    mm = (mm << 1) | g
    g = 0
    ee = ee - 1
  Wend
  While ee < -126
    st = st | g
    g = mm & 1
    mm = mm >> 1
    ee = ee + 1
  Wend
  If g = 1
    If st = 1
      mm = mm + 1
    Else
      If (mm & 1) = 1
        mm = mm + 1
      EndIf
    EndIf
  EndIf
  If mm >= 16777216
    mm = mm >> 1
    ee = ee + 1
  EndIf
  If ee > 127
    ProcedureReturn (s << 31) | 2139095040
  EndIf
  If mm >= 8388608
    ProcedureReturn (s << 31) | ((ee + 127) << 23) | (mm - 8388608)
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn (s << 31) | mm
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  MathFAddB(ba, bb) - binary32 addition, correctly rounded.
;
;  THE ALIGNMENT IS DONE IN A 57-BIT FIELD: both significands are shifted
;  LEFT by 32 before the smaller is shifted right by the exponent
;  difference. That is what makes the analysis below true, and the
;  analysis is what lets the subtraction be exact without a wider type:
;
;    d <= 32   nothing is lost. mb << 32 has 32 zero bits underneath, so
;              shifting right by at most 32 cannot drop a set bit, and
;              sticky is always 0 in this range.
;    33..56    bits can be lost, so sticky can be set - but then the
;              aligned operand is below 2^23 while the larger one is at
;              least 2^55, so they can never be equal.
;    >= 57     the aligned operand is nothing but sticky.
;
;  Therefore `pa = pb` with a sticky set is unreachable, which is the one
;  case a naive subtract-with-sticky gets wrong. And when sticky IS set
;  during a subtraction the true result lies strictly between pa-pb-1 and
;  pa-pb, so subtracting the extra 1 and keeping the sticky flag is exact
;  for rounding purposes - the retained bits are then M's own and the
;  remainder is known nonzero.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i MathFAddB(ba.i, bb.i)
  Protected sa.i
  Protected sb.i
  Protected ea.i
  Protected eb.i
  Protected ma.i
  Protected mb.i
  Protected pa.i
  Protected pb.i
  Protected d.i
  Protected st.i
  Protected s.i
  Protected mv.i
  Protected t.i
  If MathFIsNaNB(ba) = 1
    ProcedureReturn 2143289344
  EndIf
  If MathFIsNaNB(bb) = 1
    ProcedureReturn 2143289344
  EndIf
  sa = MathFSignB(ba)
  sb = MathFSignB(bb)
  If MathFIsInfB(ba) = 1
    If MathFIsInfB(bb) = 1
      If sa <> sb
        ProcedureReturn 2143289344      ; (+Inf) + (-Inf)
      EndIf
    EndIf
    ProcedureReturn ba
  EndIf
  If MathFIsInfB(bb) = 1
    ProcedureReturn bb
  EndIf
  If MathFIsZeroB(ba) = 1
    If MathFIsZeroB(bb) = 1
      If sa = sb
        ProcedureReturn ba              ; (-0) + (-0) is -0
      EndIf
      ProcedureReturn 0                 ; (+0) + (-0) is +0
    EndIf
    ProcedureReturn bb
  EndIf
  If MathFIsZeroB(bb) = 1
    ProcedureReturn ba
  EndIf
  ea = MathFExpB(ba)
  eb = MathFExpB(bb)
  ma = MathFMantB(ba)
  mb = MathFMantB(bb)
  If ea < eb
    t = ea
    ea = eb
    eb = t
    t = ma
    ma = mb
    mb = t
    t = sa
    sa = sb
    sb = t
  EndIf
  d = ea - eb
  pa = ma << 32
  st = 0
  If d >= 57
    pb = 0
    st = 1
  Else
    t = mb << 32
    pb = t >> d
    If (pb << d) <> t
      st = 1
    EndIf
  EndIf
  If sa = sb
    mv = pa + pb
    s = sa
  Else
    If pa > pb
      mv = pa - pb
      s = sa
      If st = 1
        mv = mv - 1
      EndIf
    Else
      If pa < pb
        mv = pb - pa
        s = sb
        st = 0
      Else
        If st = 0
          ProcedureReturn 0             ; exact cancellation is +0
        EndIf
        mv = 0
        s = sb
      EndIf
    EndIf
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn MathFRound(s, ea - 32, mv, st)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  MathFMulB(ba, bb) - binary32 multiplication, correctly rounded.
;  The 24x24 product is 48 bits and therefore EXACT in a 64-bit integer,
;  so there is no sticky and no error before the rounder.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i MathFMulB(ba.i, bb.i)
  Protected s.i
  If MathFIsNaNB(ba) = 1
    ProcedureReturn 2143289344
  EndIf
  If MathFIsNaNB(bb) = 1
    ProcedureReturn 2143289344
  EndIf
  s = MathFSignB(ba) ! MathFSignB(bb)
  If MathFIsInfB(ba) = 1
    If MathFIsZeroB(bb) = 1
      ProcedureReturn 2143289344        ; Inf * 0
    EndIf
    ProcedureReturn (s << 31) | 2139095040
  EndIf
  If MathFIsInfB(bb) = 1
    If MathFIsZeroB(ba) = 1
      ProcedureReturn 2143289344
    EndIf
    ProcedureReturn (s << 31) | 2139095040
  EndIf
  If MathFIsZeroB(ba) = 1
    ProcedureReturn s << 31
  EndIf
  If MathFIsZeroB(bb) = 1
    ProcedureReturn s << 31
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn MathFRound(s, MathFExpB(ba) + MathFExpB(bb) - 23, MathFMantB(ba) * MathFMantB(bb), 0)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  MathFDivB(ba, bb) - binary32 division, correctly rounded.
;  The numerator is pre-shifted by 30 so the quotient carries at least 30
;  significant bits whatever the operands, and the REMAINDER is the
;  sticky - which is exactly the information the rounder needs and the
;  reason this is a single divide rather than a bit loop.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i MathFDivB(ba.i, bb.i)
  Protected s.i
  Protected n.i
  Protected q.i
  Protected r.i
  Protected st.i
  If MathFIsNaNB(ba) = 1
    ProcedureReturn 2143289344
  EndIf
  If MathFIsNaNB(bb) = 1
    ProcedureReturn 2143289344
  EndIf
  s = MathFSignB(ba) ! MathFSignB(bb)
  If MathFIsInfB(ba) = 1
    If MathFIsInfB(bb) = 1
      ProcedureReturn 2143289344        ; Inf / Inf
    EndIf
    ProcedureReturn (s << 31) | 2139095040
  EndIf
  If MathFIsInfB(bb) = 1
    ProcedureReturn s << 31
  EndIf
  If MathFIsZeroB(bb) = 1
    If MathFIsZeroB(ba) = 1
      ProcedureReturn 2143289344        ; 0 / 0
    EndIf
    ProcedureReturn (s << 31) | 2139095040
  EndIf
  If MathFIsZeroB(ba) = 1
    ProcedureReturn s << 31
  EndIf
  n = MathFMantB(ba) << 30
  q = n / MathFMantB(bb)
  r = n % MathFMantB(bb)
  st = 0
  If r <> 0
    st = 1
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn MathFRound(s, MathFExpB(ba) - MathFExpB(bb) - 7, q, st)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  MathFCmpB(ba, bb) - -1, 0 or 1, and -2 for UNORDERED (either is NaN).
;
;  -2 rather than a fourth procedure, because every caller has to handle
;  the NaN case explicitly and a boolean answer would let it be ignored.
;  Note that -2 is also "less than", so do not write `If MathFCmpB(...)
;  < 0` and mean it - use the MathFLt/MathFGt wrappers below, which
;  answer 0 for unordered.
;
;  The ordering trick is the same one sort.pi4 uses on float bit
;  patterns: a positive float's pattern already sorts correctly as an
;  integer, and a negative one is mapped to the negation of its
;  magnitude bits, which puts -0.0 and +0.0 both at 0.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i MathFCmpB(ba.i, bb.i)
  Protected ka.i
  Protected kb.i
  If MathFIsNaNB(ba) = 1
    ProcedureReturn -2
  EndIf
  If MathFIsNaNB(bb) = 1
    ProcedureReturn -2
  EndIf
  ka = ba
  If (ka >> 31) = 1
    ka = 0 - (ka & 2147483647)
  EndIf
  kb = bb
  If (kb >> 31) = 1
    kb = 0 - (kb & 2147483647)
  EndIf
  If ka < kb
    ProcedureReturn -1
  EndIf
  If ka > kb
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  MathFFromIntB / MathFToIntB - the conversions the backend would
;  normally emit for `nf = n` and `n = u`.
;
;  FromInt rounds to nearest even, which is what every hardware
;  conversion does and what the reductions in Exp() and Log() assume.
;  ToInt TRUNCATES TOWARD ZERO, which is the language's rule, and
;  saturates at +/-(2^63-1) rather than wrapping - a wrapped answer looks
;  like a real one.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i MathFFromIntB(n.i)
  If n = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;  THE ONE INPUT THAT HAS NO NEGATION, AND WHAT IT USED TO DO
  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;  BUG FOUND 2026-08-26, by the differential gate's vector list before
  ;  the gate was ever run on hardware. This procedure took the
  ;  magnitude of a negative operand with `0 - n`, and there is exactly
  ;  one 64-bit value for which that does not produce a positive number:
  ;  -9223372036854775808, whose magnitude 2^63 is not representable.
  ;  `0 - n` wraps straight back to n, still negative.
  ;
  ;  IT DID NOT RETURN A WRONG NUMBER. IT DID NOT RETURN AT ALL.
  ;  MathFRound below received a negative mantissa, skipped its `down`
  ;  loop (the test is `mm >= 16777216`), entered its `up` loop
  ;  (`mm < 8388608`, true for anything negative), and on the first
  ;  iteration `mm << 1` overflowed a negative power of two to exactly
  ;  zero. Zero is still less than 8388608, and shifting zero left
  ;  produces zero, so the loop spins forever with the exponent
  ;  decrementing into nothing. A hang, in a maths library, from one
  ;  legal argument.
  ;
  ;  Nothing had ever called it with that value. That is the whole
  ;  reason a differential gate is worth building: the value went into
  ;  the vector table because it is an obvious boundary, and the bug was
  ;  found by writing down what SHOULD happen, before any hardware ran.
  ;
  ;  THE ANSWER IS EXACT, which is worth saying because it looks like a
  ;  special case being fudged. 2^63 is a power of two, so it is exactly
  ;  representable in binary32: significand 2^23 with an exponent of 63,
  ;  which is what MathFRound is handed below. The result is $DF000000
  ;  and there is no rounding involved at all.
  ;
  ;  MathFRound's PRECONDITION is now stated in its own header: the
  ;  mantissa must be positive. This is the only call site in the file
  ;  that could ever have violated it - the adder's operand is a sum of
  ;  two values below 2^56, the multiplier's is a product of two values
  ;  below 2^24, and the divider's is a quotient of a value below 2^54.
  If n = -9223372036854775808
    ProcedureReturn MathFRound(1, 63, 8388608, 0)
  EndIf
  If n < 0
    ProcedureReturn MathFRound(1, 23, 0 - n, 0)
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn MathFRound(0, 23, n, 0)
EndProcedure

Procedure.i MathFToIntB(b.i)
  Protected e.i
  Protected m.i
  Protected v.i
  If MathFIsNaNB(b) = 1
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If MathFIsZeroB(b) = 1
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  e = MathFExpB(b)
  m = MathFMantB(b)
  If MathFIsInfB(b) = 1
    e = 200
  EndIf
  If e < 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If e > 62
    If MathFSignB(b) = 1
      ProcedureReturn -9223372036854775807
    EndIf
    ProcedureReturn 9223372036854775807
  EndIf
  If e >= 23
    v = m << (e - 23)
  Else
    v = m >> (23 - e)
  EndIf
  If MathFSignB(b) = 1
    ProcedureReturn 0 - v
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn v
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  THE .f-FACING WRAPPERS
;
;  These exist so that the ported bodies below read as close to the
;  originals as this target allows, and so that the day the emitter can
;  do floats there is exactly one kind of edit to make. They are PUBLIC:
;  a caller who needs to add two floats on this part needs them too,
;  because `a + b` does not build.
; ======================================================================
Procedure.f MathFAdd(a.f, b.f)
  ProcedureReturn MathFromBits(MathFAddB(MathBitsOf(a), MathBitsOf(b)))
EndProcedure

Procedure.f MathFSub(a.f, b.f)
  ; Subtraction is addition with the subtrahend's sign bit flipped, which
  ; is exact and also correct for a NaN (a NaN with either sign is still
  ; a NaN and MathFAddB canonicalises it).
  ProcedureReturn MathFromBits(MathFAddB(MathBitsOf(a), MathBitsOf(b) ! 2147483648))
EndProcedure

Procedure.f MathFMul(a.f, b.f)
  ProcedureReturn MathFromBits(MathFMulB(MathBitsOf(a), MathBitsOf(b)))
EndProcedure

Procedure.f MathFDiv(a.f, b.f)
  ProcedureReturn MathFromBits(MathFDivB(MathBitsOf(a), MathBitsOf(b)))
EndProcedure

; MathFAbs(x) - |x|, by clearing one bit. Exact for every input
; including NaN and the infinities, and the reason Abs() is still absent
; from the public namespace: this one cannot collide with wiring's abs().
Procedure.f MathFAbs(x.f)
  ProcedureReturn MathFromBits(MathBitsOf(x) & 2147483647)
EndProcedure

Procedure.f MathFNeg(x.f)
  ProcedureReturn MathFromBits(MathBitsOf(x) ! 2147483648)
EndProcedure

; The relational wrappers. Each answers 0 for an unordered comparison, so
; `If MathFLt(x, 0.0) = 1` is false for a NaN - which is what the IEEE
; relation means and what every ported body below relies on.
Procedure.i MathFCmp(a.f, b.f)
  ProcedureReturn MathFCmpB(MathBitsOf(a), MathBitsOf(b))
EndProcedure

Procedure.i MathFLt(a.f, b.f)
  If MathFCmpB(MathBitsOf(a), MathBitsOf(b)) = -1
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

Procedure.i MathFGt(a.f, b.f)
  If MathFCmpB(MathBitsOf(a), MathBitsOf(b)) = 1
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

Procedure.i MathFEq(a.f, b.f)
  If MathFCmpB(MathBitsOf(a), MathBitsOf(b)) = 0
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

Procedure.i MathFLe(a.f, b.f)
  Protected c.i
  c = MathFCmpB(MathBitsOf(a), MathBitsOf(b))
  If c = -1
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  If c = 0
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

Procedure.i MathFGe(a.f, b.f)
  Protected c.i
  c = MathFCmpB(MathBitsOf(a), MathBitsOf(b))
  If c = 1
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  If c = 0
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

; MathFFromInt / MathFToInt - the .f-facing conversions. MathFToInt is
; the UNCLAMPED 64-bit float-to-integer; Int() below is the documented
; 32-bit one that saturates, and they are deliberately two functions.
Procedure.f MathFFromInt(n.i)
  ProcedureReturn MathFromBits(MathFFromIntB(n))
EndProcedure

Procedure.i MathFToInt(x.f)
  ProcedureReturn MathFToIntB(MathBitsOf(x))
EndProcedure


; ======================================================================
;  THE PUBLIC MATHS SURFACE - the desktop BASIC's names and semantics
; ======================================================================

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; FloatBits(x) - the raw IEEE-754 binary32 bit pattern of x, as a SIGNED
; 32-bit value. Nothing is converted: FloatBits(1.5) is 1069547520, not 1.
; This is how every classification in this file is done - a NaN or an
; infinity has to be recognised from its bits, because arithmetic on one
; produces another one and tells you nothing.
;
; IT STAYS SIGNED, and on a 64-bit target that is now a visible choice
; rather than a consequence. FloatBits(-1.0) is -1082130432 here exactly
; as it is on the Picos, so a program moved between them keeps working
; and IsInfinity()'s `b = -8388608` still reads the same. The sign
; extension is written out by hand below rather than left to the return
; type, so it does not depend on how the backend narrows a .l return.
; If you want the unsigned pattern, mask with $FFFFFFFF - or use
; MathBitsOf(), which is the internal spelling and returns 0..4294967295.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.l FloatBits(x.f)
  Protected b.i
  b = MathBitsOf(x)
  If b >= 2147483648
    ProcedureReturn b - 4294967296
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn b
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; FloatFromBits(b) - the binary32 value whose bit pattern is b. The exact
; inverse of FloatBits, and again nothing is converted: FloatFromBits(1)
; is the smallest subnormal, not 1.0. Either spelling of a pattern works
; - -8388608 and 4286578688 are the same four bytes.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.f FloatFromBits(b.i)
  ProcedureReturn MathFromBits(b)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; Infinity() - positive infinity, 0x7F800000. Negate it for the other one.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.f Infinity()
  ProcedureReturn MathFromBits(2139095040)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; NaN() - a quiet Not-a-Number. Every domain error in this file returns
; this one bit pattern, 0x7FC00000.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.f NaN()
  ProcedureReturn MathFromBits(2143289344)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; IsNaN(x) - 1 if x is Not-a-Number, else 0.
;
; The RP2350 file writes this as `If x <> x`, using the property that a
; NaN compares unequal to itself. THAT DOES NOT COMPILE HERE - a float
; comparison is refused by the emitter - so it is a bit test instead,
; which is what the `x <> x` was standing in for anyway. Do NOT test for
; a NaN with '=' in your own code either, on this target or any other.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.l IsNaN(x.f)
  ProcedureReturn MathFIsNaNB(MathBitsOf(x))
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; IsInfinity(x) - 1 for +Infinity, -1 for -Infinity, 0 for anything else
; including NaN. The desktop BASIC only promises "nonzero", so a program
; that just tests truth is portable; the sign is extra.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.l IsInfinity(x.f)
  Protected b.i
  b = MathBitsOf(x)
  If b = 2139095040
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  If b = 4286578688                 ; 0xFF800000
    ProcedureReturn -1
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; Sign(x) - -1 if x is negative, +1 if positive, 0 if zero.
; Returns an INTEGER, as the desktop's does. Sign(-0.0) is 0 and
; Sign(NaN) is 0; both were checked against the desktop rather than
; assumed. Done on the bits because the float comparisons the source used
; are refused on this target - and it is exact either way.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.l Sign(x.f)
  Protected b.i
  b = MathBitsOf(x)
  If MathFIsNaNB(b) = 1             ; not positive, not negative
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If (b & 2147483647) = 0           ; +0.0 and -0.0 alike
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If (b >> 31) = 1
    ProcedureReturn -1
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; Int(x) - the integer part of x, TRUNCATED toward zero. Int(10.565) is
; 10 and Int(-10.565) is -10, which is the desktop's rule and NOT the
; older BASIC floor() rule that would give -11. There is no rounding.
;
; TWO DIFFERENCES FROM THE DESKTOP, both inherited from the RP2350 file:
;   - the result is 32-bit. A magnitude beyond 2147483647 saturates at
;     +/-2147483647 rather than wrapping, because a wrapped answer looks
;     like a real one.
;   - Int(NaN) is 0, where the desktop hands back the CPU's
;     integer-indefinite value.
;
; THE 32-BIT BOUND IS KEPT ON PURPOSE HERE, even though .i on this target
; is 64 bits and would hold the wider answer. Widening a documented
; function's range during a port makes the same source disagree with
; itself between two chips, silently, for arguments in between. If you
; want the wider answer on this part, MathFToInt(x) is unclamped and
; returns .i.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.l Int(x.f)
  Protected v.i
  If MathFIsNaNB(MathBitsOf(x)) = 1
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  v = MathFToIntB(MathBitsOf(x))
  If v > 2147483647
    ProcedureReturn 2147483647
  EndIf
  If v < -2147483647
    ProcedureReturn -2147483647
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn v
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; Degree(x) - an angle in radians converted to degrees. Degree(#PI/4) is
; 45.0. There is NO normalisation: an input above 2*pi gives a result
; above 360, and a negative input gives a negative result, exactly as the
; desktop does.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.f Degree(x.f)
  ProcedureReturn MathFMul(x, 57.29577951308232)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; Radian(x) - an angle in degrees converted to radians. Radian(90) is
; 1.5707964. No normalisation, same as Degree().
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.f Radian(x.f)
  ProcedureReturn MathFMul(x, 0.017453292519943295)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; MathNearestInt(t) - t rounded to the NEAREST integer, halves away from
; zero. Used by the range reductions below, where an off-by-one quadrant
; is a sign error rather than a rounding error.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.l MathNearestInt(t.f)
  Protected u.f
  If MathFGe(t, 0.0) = 1
    u = MathFAdd(t, 0.5)
  Else
    u = MathFSub(t, 0.5)
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn MathFToIntB(MathBitsOf(u))
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; MathPow2i(n) - 2 raised to an INTEGER power, built directly from the
; exponent field rather than by multiplying. Exact for -126 <= n <= 127;
; 0.0 below that and +Infinity above. Exp() and Sqr() use it to apply a
; power-of-two scaling, which is the one scaling that costs no accuracy
; at all - and on this target it is also the one that costs no soft-float
; call, since it is pure integer work.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.f MathPow2i(n.l)
  If n > 127
    ProcedureReturn MathFromBits(2139095040)
  EndIf
  If n < -126
    ProcedureReturn 0.0
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn MathFromBits((n + 127) << 23)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; Sqr(x) - the square root of x.
;
;     Sqr(-1)     NaN          Sqr(0.0)     0.0
;     Sqr(-0.0)   -0.0         Sqr(+Inf)    +Infinity
;
; METHOD. A first estimate is read straight out of the exponent field:
; halving a float's bit pattern halves its exponent, which is most of a
; square root already, and the additive constant 532487669 is the offset
; that minimises the leading error of what is left (about 3.5%). Three
; Newton-Raphson steps y = (y + x/y)/2 follow, each roughly doubling the
; correct digits: 3.5% -> 6e-4 -> 2e-7 -> below one ULP. A fourth step
; would change nothing a binary32 can hold.
;
; A SUBNORMAL INPUT IS SCALED FIRST. Below 1.18e-38 the exponent field is
; 0 and the bit trick has nothing to halve, so the estimate is garbage
; and Newton converges to garbage - silently, because every step still
; looks like it is converging. Multiplying by 2^40 first and the result
; by 2^-20 is exact both ways (powers of two) and puts the value back in
; the range the estimate was fitted for.
;
; THREE DIVISIONS is what this costs, and on this target a division is
; the most expensive thing the soft core does. It is still the right
; algorithm: a division-free Newton on the reciprocal square root needs
; more steps to reach the same ULP and the extra multiplies cost more
; than the divisions saved.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.f Sqr(x.f)
  Protected b.i
  Protected v.f
  Protected y.f
  Protected q.f
  Protected scale.f
  If MathFIsNaNB(MathBitsOf(x)) = 1   ; NaN in, NaN out
    ProcedureReturn x
  EndIf
  If MathFLt(x, 0.0) = 1
    ProcedureReturn MathFromBits(2143289344)
  EndIf
  If MathFEq(x, 0.0) = 1                  ; returns x, so -0.0 stays -0.0
    ProcedureReturn x
  EndIf
  b = MathBitsOf(x)
  If b = 2139095040                   ; +Infinity
    ProcedureReturn x
  EndIf
  v = x
  scale = 1.0
  If b < 8388608                      ; subnormal - see the note above
    v = MathFMul(v, 1099511627776.0)  ; 2^40
    scale = 0.00000095367431640625    ; 2^-20
    b = MathBitsOf(v)
  EndIf
  y = MathFromBits((b >> 1) + 532487669)
  q = MathFDiv(v, y)
  y = MathFMul(MathFAdd(y, q), 0.5)
  q = MathFDiv(v, y)
  y = MathFMul(MathFAdd(y, q), 0.5)
  q = MathFDiv(v, y)
  y = MathFMul(MathFAdd(y, q), 0.5)
  ProcedureReturn MathFMul(y, scale)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; Exp(x) - e raised to the power x. The inverse of Log().
;
;     Exp(0)      1.0          Exp(1000)    +Infinity
;     Exp(-1000)  0.0          Exp(NaN)     NaN
;
; METHOD. x is split as x = n*ln2 + r with n whole and |r| <= ln2/2, so
; e^x = 2^n * e^r and only the small factor needs a polynomial. ln2 is
; carried in two pieces - 0.693359375 is 355/512, nine significant bits,
; so n*it is EXACT for every n that can occur here, and the tiny
; correction 0.00021219444005469057 restores the rest. Subtracting one
; exact product loses nothing; subtracting a single rounded ln2 would
; lose about a bit per unit of |x|, which is the usual way this function
; is got wrong.
;
; e^r on |r| <= 0.3466 is a degree-7 minimax polynomial evaluated as
; 1 + r + p(r)*r^2, where p is the degree-5 Horner chain below - which
; keeps the two dominant terms exact and lets the polynomial carry only
; the small remainder.
;
; The 2^n factor is applied last with MathPow2i, one exact multiply. When
; n is below -126 the answer is subnormal and 2^n is not representable,
; so the scaling is done in two steps rather than flushed to zero -
; without that, exp(-90) would come out as 0 instead of 8.2e-40.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.f Exp(x.f)
  Protected n.l
  Protected t.f
  Protected nf.f
  Protected r.f
  Protected z.f
  Protected p.f
  Protected v.f
  If MathFIsNaNB(MathBitsOf(x)) = 1
    ProcedureReturn x
  EndIf
  If MathFGt(x, 88.72283) = 1             ; the largest x with a finite e^x
    ProcedureReturn MathFromBits(2139095040)
  EndIf
  If MathFLt(x, -103.9721) = 1            ; below the smallest subnormal
    ProcedureReturn 0.0
  EndIf
  t = MathFMul(x, 1.4426950408889634) ; x / ln2
  n = MathNearestInt(t)
  nf = MathFFromInt(n)
  r = MathFSub(x, MathFMul(nf, 0.693359375))          ; exact: 355/512
  r = MathFAdd(r, MathFMul(nf, 0.00021219444005469057))
  p = 0.000198756915
  p = MathFAdd(MathFMul(p, r), 0.0013981999507)
  p = MathFAdd(MathFMul(p, r), 0.0083334519073)
  p = MathFAdd(MathFMul(p, r), 0.041665795894)
  p = MathFAdd(MathFMul(p, r), 0.16666665459)
  p = MathFAdd(MathFMul(p, r), 0.50000001201)
  z = MathFMul(r, r)
  v = MathFAdd(1.0, r)
  v = MathFAdd(v, MathFMul(p, z))
  If n < -126
    v = MathFMul(v, MathPow2i(n + 60))
    ProcedureReturn MathFMul(v, MathPow2i(-60))
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn MathFMul(v, MathPow2i(n))
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; Log(x) - the natural logarithm (base e) of x. The inverse of Exp().
;
;     Log(1)      0.0          Log(0)       -Infinity
;     Log(-1)     NaN          Log(+Inf)    +Infinity
;
; METHOD. x is split into m * 2^e with m in [sqrt(1/2), sqrt(2)) - the
; exponent comes straight out of the bit pattern, so that half is exact -
; and log(x) = e*ln2 + log(m). log(m) is then evaluated as the atanh
; series in s = (m-1)/(m+1):
;
;     log(m) = 2s * (1 + s^2/3 + s^4/5 + s^6/7 + s^8/9)
;
; which is used instead of a series in (m-1) because |s| <= 0.1716 while
; |m-1| can reach 0.414 - the terms fall away about six times faster, so
; five of them reach 7e-10 where a direct series would need fifteen.
; Centring m on 1 rather than leaving it in [1,2) is what buys that; it
; costs one comparison and one exact halving.
;
; A SUBNORMAL INPUT is scaled up by 2^40 first and 40*ln2 subtracted from
; the exponent term afterwards, for the same reason as in Sqr(): a
; subnormal has no exponent field to read.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.f Log(x.f)
  Protected b.i
  Protected e.l
  Protected v.f
  Protected m.f
  Protected s.f
  Protected z.f
  Protected p.f
  Protected ef.f
  If MathFIsNaNB(MathBitsOf(x)) = 1
    ProcedureReturn x
  EndIf
  If MathFLt(x, 0.0) = 1
    ProcedureReturn MathFromBits(2143289344)
  EndIf
  If MathFEq(x, 0.0) = 1
    ProcedureReturn MathFromBits(4286578688)          ; -Infinity
  EndIf
  b = MathBitsOf(x)
  If b = 2139095040
    ProcedureReturn x
  EndIf
  v = x
  e = 0
  If b < 8388608                      ; subnormal - see the note above
    v = MathFMul(v, 1099511627776.0)  ; 2^40
    e = -40
    b = MathBitsOf(v)
  EndIf
  e = e + ((b >> 23) & 255) - 127
  m = MathFromBits((b & 8388607) | 1065353216)   ; mantissa, exponent 0
  If MathFGt(m, 1.4142135623730951) = 1
    m = MathFMul(m, 0.5)
    e = e + 1
  EndIf
  s = MathFDiv(MathFSub(m, 1.0), MathFAdd(m, 1.0))
  z = MathFMul(s, s)
  p = 0.1111111111111111
  p = MathFAdd(MathFMul(p, z), 0.14285714285714285)
  p = MathFAdd(MathFMul(p, z), 0.2)
  p = MathFAdd(MathFMul(p, z), 0.3333333333333333)
  p = MathFAdd(MathFMul(p, z), 1.0)
  ef = MathFFromInt(e)
  ProcedureReturn MathFAdd(MathFMul(ef, 0.6931471805599453), MathFMul(MathFMul(2.0, s), p))
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; MathSinCos(x, wantCos) - the shared core of Sin() and Cos().
; wantCos of 0 gives sin(x), anything else gives cos(x).
;
; Both are one function because the argument reduction IS the function -
; the polynomial afterwards is a few instructions and the reduction is
; everything that decides whether the answer is right. Two copies of it
; would be two things to keep in step, and the one that drifts would go
; wrong only at large arguments, which is exactly where nobody looks.
;
; A cosine is a sine one quadrant along, so wantCos simply adds 1 to the
; quadrant count. The quadrant then selects sin(r) or cos(r) of the
; reduced argument and whether to negate it.
;
; `n & 3` ON A 64-BIT SIGNED n. Checked, because the brief for this port
; said to be suspicious of exactly this shape: n is negative for negative
; arguments and `-1 & 3` is 3 in two's complement at every width, so the
; quadrant selection is unaffected by .i having grown. A mask that keeps
; only low bits is one of the few bit operations that does not care.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.f MathSinCos(x.f, wantCos.l)
  Protected a.f
  Protected t.f
  Protected nf.f
  Protected r.f
  Protected z.f
  Protected p.f
  Protected v.f
  Protected n.l
  Protected q.l
  If MathFIsNaNB(MathBitsOf(x)) = 1
    ProcedureReturn x
  EndIf
  a = MathFAbs(x)
  If MathFGt(a, 12867.0) = 1              ; reduction stops being exact
    ProcedureReturn MathFromBits(2143289344)
  EndIf
  t = MathFMul(x, 0.6366197723675814) ; x * 2/pi - which quadrant
  n = MathNearestInt(t)
  nf = MathFFromInt(n)
  r = MathFSub(x, MathFMul(nf, 1.5703125))    ; pi/2 in three exact pieces
  r = MathFSub(r, MathFMul(nf, 0.0004837512969970703125))
  r = MathFSub(r, MathFMul(nf, 0.0000000754979012640433))
  q = n & 3
  If wantCos <> 0
    q = (q + 1) & 3
  EndIf
  z = MathFMul(r, r)
  If q = 1 Or q = 3
    ; cos(r) = 1 - r^2/2 + r^4/24 - ... , Taylor to r^10; the truncated
    ; term is (pi/4)^12/479001600, about 1.2e-10, well under one ULP.
    p = -0.0000002755731922398589
    p = MathFAdd(MathFMul(p, z), 0.0000248015873015873)
    p = MathFAdd(MathFMul(p, z), -0.001388888888888889)
    p = MathFAdd(MathFMul(p, z), 0.041666666666666664)
    p = MathFAdd(MathFMul(p, z), -0.5)
    v = MathFAdd(1.0, MathFMul(p, z))
  Else
    ; sin(r) = r - r^3/6 + r^5/120 - ... , Taylor to r^9; the truncated
    ; term is (pi/4)^11/39916800, about 1.9e-09.
    p = 0.0000027557319223985893
    p = MathFAdd(MathFMul(p, z), -0.0001984126984126984)
    p = MathFAdd(MathFMul(p, z), 0.008333333333333333)
    p = MathFAdd(MathFMul(p, z), -0.16666666666666666)
    v = MathFAdd(r, MathFMul(r, MathFMul(p, z)))
  EndIf
  If q >= 2
    ProcedureReturn MathFSub(0.0, v)
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn v
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; Sin(x) - the sine of an angle given in RADIANS. Use Radian() to convert
; from degrees. The result is between -1.0 and 1.0.
;
;     Sin(0)          0.0
;     Sin(Radian(30)) 0.5
;     Sin(NaN)        NaN
;     |x| > 12867     NaN     - a refusal, not a failure. See TRIG above.
;
; Within 0.001 radians of a multiple of pi the ABSOLUTE error is about
; 1.4e-10 and the ULP count is meaningless: the answer is near zero, so
; one ULP of it is an enormous number of steps. If you are testing sin(x)
; for a zero crossing, compare against a small absolute tolerance and not
; against 0.0 - which is true of every implementation of these functions
; and not a property of this one.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.f Sin(x.f)
  ProcedureReturn MathSinCos(x, 0)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; Cos(x) - the cosine of an angle given in RADIANS. Result -1.0 to 1.0.
; Same accuracy, same 12867-radian refusal and same reasoning as Sin().
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.f Cos(x.f)
  ProcedureReturn MathSinCos(x, 1)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; Tan(x) - the tangent of an angle given in RADIANS.
;
; Computed as Sin(x)/Cos(x), and the consequences of that are worth
; stating rather than hiding. Near a pole (an odd multiple of pi/2) the
; cosine is tiny and its few remaining significant bits decide the whole
; answer, so accuracy degrades steadily as you approach one - away from a
; pole this is about 3 ULP, at 1e-4 radians from a pole it is nearer 1e-3
; relative. That is the identity's arithmetic, not a defect, and the
; desktop BASIC behaves the same way.
;
; Cos(x) can never be exactly 0 for a representable x, since pi/2 is not
; representable - but if it ever were, the answer would be the correctly
; signed Infinity, which is also right.
;
;     Tan(0)       0.0        |x| > 12867   NaN
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.f Tan(x.f)
  Protected s.f
  Protected c.f
  s = MathSinCos(x, 0)
  c = MathSinCos(x, 1)
  ProcedureReturn MathFDiv(s, c)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; MathATan(t) - arctangent of t, in radians, over the whole real line.
; Result between -pi/2 and +pi/2. Internal; ATan2() is the public one.
;
; The argument is folded twice before the polynomial so that it only ever
; has to cover |z| <= tan(pi/8) = 0.4142:
;
;     |t| > 2.4142   z = -1/t      and add pi/2   (atan t = pi/2 - atan 1/t)
;     |t| > 0.4142   z = (t-1)/(t+1) and add pi/4
;
; Both identities are exact rearrangements, and folding twice is what
; makes a four-coefficient minimax fit enough. Without it the series near
; |t| = 1 converges so slowly that fifteen terms would not do.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.f MathATan(t.f)
  Protected a.f
  Protected z.f
  Protected w.f
  Protected p.f
  Protected y0.f
  Protected v.f
  Protected neg.l
  neg = 0
  a = t
  If MathFLt(a, 0.0) = 1
    a = MathFSub(0.0, a)
    neg = 1
  EndIf
  If MathFGt(a, 2.414213562373095) = 1
    z = MathFDiv(-1.0, a)
    y0 = 1.5707963267948966
  ElseIf MathFGt(a, 0.4142135623730950) = 1
    z = MathFDiv(MathFSub(a, 1.0), MathFAdd(a, 1.0))
    y0 = 0.7853981633974483
  Else
    z = a
    y0 = 0.0
  EndIf
  w = MathFMul(z, z)
  p = 0.0805374449538
  p = MathFAdd(MathFMul(p, w), -0.138776856032)
  p = MathFAdd(MathFMul(p, w), 0.199777106478)
  p = MathFAdd(MathFMul(p, w), -0.333329491539)
  v = MathFAdd(y0, MathFAdd(z, MathFMul(z, MathFMul(p, w))))
  If neg <> 0
    ProcedureReturn MathFSub(0.0, v)
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn v
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; ATan2(x, y) - the angle in radians between the x axis and the direction
; (x, y). Result between -pi and +pi.
;
; ================ THE X COMES FIRST. READ THIS TWICE. =================
; The desktop BASIC spells this ATan2(x, y) and computes the arctangent
; of y/x. Every C library, and everything that copies C, spells the same
; function atan2(y, x) - the arguments the other way round. This file
; follows the desktop, because that is the language these names come
; from. Swapping them does not fail; it silently reflects every angle
; about the 45-degree line, which is a bug people find on a robot rather
; than on a bench.
;
;     ATan2(1, 0)   0.0            ATan2(0, 1)    +pi/2
;     ATan2(-1, 0)  +pi            ATan2(0, -1)   -pi/2
;     ATan2(10, 10) +pi/4          ATan2(0, 0)    0.0
;
; ATan2(0, 0) is 0.0, which is a DEFINITION, not a computation - the
; direction of a zero-length vector is not a question with an answer, and
; 0.0 is the answer the desktop gives, so it is the answer given here.
;
; Infinite arguments are handled explicitly rather than left to fall out
; of the division: y/x with both infinite is a NaN, and a NaN there would
; be a wrong answer for a direction that is perfectly well defined.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.f ATan2(x.f, y.f)
  Protected ix.l
  Protected iy.l
  Protected r.f
  If MathFIsNaNB(MathBitsOf(x)) = 1
    ProcedureReturn x
  EndIf
  If MathFIsNaNB(MathBitsOf(y)) = 1
    ProcedureReturn y
  EndIf
  ix = IsInfinity(x)
  iy = IsInfinity(y)
  If ix <> 0 Or iy <> 0
    If ix > 0 And iy = 0
      ProcedureReturn 0.0
    EndIf
    If ix < 0 And iy = 0
      If MathFLt(y, 0.0) = 1
        ProcedureReturn -3.141592653589793
      EndIf
      ProcedureReturn 3.141592653589793
    EndIf
    If ix = 0 And iy > 0
      ProcedureReturn 1.5707963267948966
    EndIf
    If ix = 0 And iy < 0
      ProcedureReturn -1.5707963267948966
    EndIf
    ; both infinite: the direction is one of the four diagonals
    If ix > 0 And iy > 0
      ProcedureReturn 0.7853981633974483
    EndIf
    If ix > 0 And iy < 0
      ProcedureReturn -0.7853981633974483
    EndIf
    If ix < 0 And iy > 0
      ProcedureReturn 2.356194490192345
    EndIf
    ProcedureReturn -2.356194490192345
  EndIf
  If MathFEq(x, 0.0) = 1
    If MathFGt(y, 0.0) = 1
      ProcedureReturn 1.5707963267948966
    EndIf
    If MathFLt(y, 0.0) = 1
      ProcedureReturn -1.5707963267948966
    EndIf
    ProcedureReturn 0.0               ; ATan2(0, 0) - defined as 0.0
  EndIf
  r = MathATan(MathFDiv(y, x))
  If MathFGt(x, 0.0) = 1
    ProcedureReturn r
  EndIf
  If MathFLt(y, 0.0) = 1
    ProcedureReturn MathFSub(r, 3.141592653589793)
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn MathFAdd(r, 3.141592653589793)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; MathPowInt(x, k) - x raised to a whole number power, by repeated
; squaring. Internal; Pow() routes to it whenever it can.
; k may be negative; k of 0 gives 1.0.
;
; `e >> 1` on a 64-bit .i: e is made non-negative on the line above, and
; an arithmetic right shift of a non-negative value is a logical one, so
; the loop terminates exactly as it did at 32 bits. Named because ">> on
; a value that might be negative" is one of the shapes this port was told
; to hunt, and this is the one place in the file it appears.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.f MathPowInt(x.f, k.l)
  Protected base.f
  Protected acc.f
  Protected e.l
  acc = 1.0
  base = x
  e = k
  If e < 0
    e = 0 - e
  EndIf
  While e > 0
    If (e & 1) <> 0
      acc = MathFMul(acc, base)
    EndIf
    base = MathFMul(base, base)
    e = e >> 1
  Wend
  If k < 0
    ProcedureReturn MathFDiv(1.0, acc)
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn acc
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; Pow(x, y) - x raised to the power y. There is no '^' operator in this
; language, which is what this function is for.
;
;     Pow(2, 10)    1024.0       Pow(0, 0)     1.0
;     Pow(-2, 3)    -8.0         Pow(0, -1)    +Infinity
;     Pow(-2, 0.5)  NaN          Pow(x, 0)     1.0, always
;     Pow(27, 0.3333333) about 3.0
;     Pow(-2, 100)  NaN          - NOT 1.27e30. See THE BOUND below.
;
; A NEGATIVE BASE NEEDS A WHOLE-NUMBER EXPONENT. Pow(-8, 1/3) is NaN
; here and NaN on the desktop: (-8)^(1/3) has no real value, and the cube
; root a reader is thinking of is a different function. This is a
; refusal, not a limitation.
;
; TWO PATHS, and which one you get changes the accuracy:
;
;   y is a whole number with |y| <= 64  ->  repeated squaring. Exact
;     where the answer is exact, and 6.06 ULP worst case otherwise
;     (measured here, |y| <= 20). This is where a negative base is
;     allowed. Pow(2,10) is exactly 1024.0.
;
;   otherwise  ->  Exp(y * Log(x)), which is the only general method
;     available and carries the error of the composition: 39.99 ULP
;     worst case, measured here over base 0.01..50 and |y| <= 20, which
;     is about 2.4e-06 relative and roughly forty times the type's own
;     resolution. That is inherent - a 1-ULP error in Log(x) is
;     multiplied by y before Exp() ever sees it - and the whole-number
;     path exists precisely because it is the case where that price need
;     not be paid.
;
; THE BOUND. For a POSITIVE base, |y| <= 64 is only a cost decision:
; x^64 already overflows binary32 for any base of 4 or more.
;
; FOR A NEGATIVE BASE IT IS A CORRECTNESS BOUND, and that is the sharp
; edge in this function. Past |y| = 64 a negative base falls through to
; the general path, which refuses it - so Pow(-2, 100) is NaN even
; though the true answer, 1.2676506e30, is an ordinary representable
; binary32. Asserted on this target, not inherited. If you need a
; negative base with a large whole exponent, call MathPowInt(x, k)
; directly: it has no bound and is what the short path uses.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.f Pow(x.f, y.f)
  Protected k.l
  Protected kf.f
  Protected a.f
  Protected r.f
  If MathFEq(y, 0.0) = 1                  ; even Pow(NaN, 0) is 1.0
    ProcedureReturn 1.0
  EndIf
  If MathFIsNaNB(MathBitsOf(x)) = 1
    ProcedureReturn x
  EndIf
  If MathFIsNaNB(MathBitsOf(y)) = 1
    ProcedureReturn y
  EndIf
  If MathFEq(x, 1.0) = 1
    ProcedureReturn 1.0
  EndIf
  ; a whole-number exponent small enough to reach by squaring
  If MathFGe(y, -64.0) = 1 And MathFLe(y, 64.0) = 1
    k = Int(y)
    kf = MathFFromInt(k)
    If MathFEq(kf, y) = 1
      If MathFEq(x, 0.0) = 1
        If k < 0
          ProcedureReturn MathFromBits(2139095040)    ; +Infinity
        EndIf
        ProcedureReturn 0.0
      EndIf
      ProcedureReturn MathPowInt(x, k)
    EndIf
  EndIf
  If MathFEq(x, 0.0) = 1
    If MathFLt(y, 0.0) = 1
      ProcedureReturn MathFromBits(2139095040)
    EndIf
    ProcedureReturn 0.0
  EndIf
  If MathFLt(x, 0.0) = 1
    ; a negative base with a fractional exponent has no real value
    ProcedureReturn MathFromBits(2143289344)
  EndIf
  a = Log(x)
  r = MathFMul(y, a)
  ProcedureReturn Exp(r)
EndProcedure
